 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we know that the bird sin offering precedes the bird burnt offering?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "The baraita's answer (בנה אב) continues in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "zevachim.90a.13",
   "daf": "90a",
   "sentenceId": 12,
   "he": "חַטַּאת הָעוֹף קוֹדֶמֶת כּוּ׳. מְנָא הָנֵי מִילֵּי? דְּתָנוּ רַבָּנַן: ״וְהִקְרִיב אֶת אֲשֶׁר לַחַטָּאת רִאשׁוֹנָה״ – מָה תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר? שֶׁאֵין תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר לְלַמֵּד שֶׁתִּקְרַב רִאשׁוֹנָה, הֲרֵי כְּבָר נֶאֱמַר: ״וְאֶת הַשֵּׁנִי יַעֲשֶׂה עֹלָה״.",
   "en": "§ The mishna states: The sacrifice of «the bird sin offering precedes» the sacrifice of the bird burnt offering. The Gemara asks: «From where are these matters» derived? The Gemara answers: This is «as the Sages taught» in a baraita with regard to the bird sin offering and the bird burnt offering that a poor sinner sacrifices instead of an animal sin offering. The verse states: «“And he shall offer that which is for the sin offering first”» (Leviticus 5:8). «Why» must «the verse state» this? «As» there is «no» need for «the verse to state» this in order «to teach that» the sin offering «is sacrificed first, as» it «is already stated: “And he shall prepare the second for a burnt offering”» (Leviticus 5:10)."
  }
 ],
